WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.040
November 8, 2016 was a good night for Donald
Trump.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:07.580
"I love this country.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.080
Thank you.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.160
Thank you very much."

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:12.300
But it was also a great night for marijuana.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.960
California, Nevada, Massachusetts, and Maine

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.720
all approved legalization of recreational cannabis.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.040
And Florida, North Dakota, and Arkansas legalized
medical marijuana.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:30.480
All told, more than half the states in America
have legalized the drug in one form or another.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.390
So how will the Trump administration deal
with pot?

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:37.730
Which, after all, is still illegal under federal
law.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:42.060
On the campaign trail, Trump seemed friendly
to the notions of medical marijuana

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.680
and leaving the rest to the states.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:46.420
"I think medical should happen.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:47.420
Don't we agree?

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:48.900
I mean, I think so.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.290
And then I really believe you should leave it up to
the states.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:53.100
It should be a state situation."

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.800
But signals from his administration have been
more mixed.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:02.420
"There's still a federal law that we need
to abide by and kind of, in terms of the medical--

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.170
when it comes to recreational marijuana."

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:08.360
"I, as you know, am dubious about marijuana."

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.820
We asked cannabis industry professionals what
they see

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.500
for the future of legal weed in the Trump era.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.440
"I see the future of the marijuana industry
under the Trump administration

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.020
as, in the short term, a little cloudy.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.100
But when I say cloudy, it doesn't mean the skies are dark,

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.820
it just means it's unclear.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:29.100
I can never figure out if it's 'Art of The Deal' or not

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.720
you know, how much of it's rhetoric and how much of it is going to actually come to pass.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:39.220
I want a little more clarity before I'm really able to give my opinion.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:43.140
Trump's attorney general, Jeff Sessions, is a long time drug warrior.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:46.960
But even he seems reluctant to commit to a full on crackdown.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.400
States I guess can pass whatever laws they choose,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.580
but I'm not sure we're going to be a better, healtheir nation,

00:01:54.580 --> 00:02:00.000
that, if we're gonna have marijuana being sold at every corner grocery store.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.900
Attorney general Sessions doesn't have to worry 'cause that's not how the industry works.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:07.800
It takes a long time to get either a municipal or county permit

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.840
and they're highly regulated stores that track seed to store.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.940
There's code, you don't open up near a school.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:20.760
The people going in to these dispensaries to purchase such product have to be of age.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:26.120
I think the sitting attorney general is an older gentleman who grew up in a certain context,

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.340
and we're all victims of our own context and history.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:34.340
So I understand his position, why he has those deep-seated feelings.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:39.220
You know, marijuana is a cure for opiate abuse, give me a break,

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.320
I mean, you know this is the kind of argument that has been made out there

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:51.880
just, almost a desperate attempt to defend the harmlessness of marijuana or even it's benefits

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:53.620
I doubt that's true.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:59.460
Much of modern science contradict's Session's dim view of marijuana's therepeutic potential.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:03.620
With The National Academies of Sciences, Engineering and Medicine

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:09.000
finding conclusive evidence that cannabis is an effective treatment for chronic pain,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.920
after surveying ten thousand scientific studies on the matter.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:16.240
Nonetheless, the federal government continues to keep marijuana

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.140
in it's most highly controlled category, schedule one.

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:24.580
And recognizes 'no currently accepted medical use'

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:27.240
citing 'high potential for abuse.'

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.400
With Sessions helming the department of justice

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.740
this classification seems unlikely to change any time soon.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:38.740
Meaning the tension between state and federal law will remain.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:42.040
When you look at it being on schedule one, it's a bit absurd

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:46.920
because the definition of schedule one is 'highly addictive and no medical uses.'

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.420
And cannabis couldn't be further from the truth on both those criteria

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:55.280
so at the end of the day the truth is marching forward.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:58.440
While crackdowns on medical cannabis seem unlikely

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:03.920
given Trump's public statements and overwhelming public support for medicinal use.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.980
What about recreational use?

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:10.220
After all, that's a distinction Trump's press secretary Sean Spicer

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.820
was careful to make in his comments.

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:16.780
The states where it's allowed in accordance with the appropriations rider

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:21.600
have set forth a process to administer and-

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:27.080
and regulate that usage versus recreational marijuana, that's a very very different subject.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:30.640
With the largest state in the union legalizing recreational weed

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:34.220
the feds would need to devote major resources to crack down.

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:37.840
And that seems like a fight that most Americans aren't up for either.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.980
With 71% of Quinnipiac poll respondents

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:44.260
opposed to the federal government enforcing laws against marijuana

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:48.880
in states that legalized medical or recreational use.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:52.420
This dissaproval of federal encroachment on state laws

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:55.660
is pretty consistent across partisan lines.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:58.540
I've always been a believer in federalism and state's rights

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:04.100
I think it's up to societies and communities to dictate for themselves

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.540
what rules they choose to govern themselves by.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:10.240
Hopefully moving forward the administration will have an open mind about it

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:16.800
and be ready to listen to see how beneficial this has been in the areas where it's been tried.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.680
If if I were in an elevator and had an opportunity to speak to Donald Trump directly

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.840
I'd probably invite him for a drink or ask him to sit down over a meal, break some bread

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:32.700
and give me the opportunity to educate him about the true data of the cannabis industry.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.980
It's a job creator, I'm not saying I'm in favor of building a wall,

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:41.300
but if you wanna build a wall, there's potentially a couple billion dollars woth in excise tax revenue.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:46.280
I don't see anyone pumping the brakes on this big industry.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.140
I see it expanding and doing better.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:50.800
And helping more people.

